  "synonym_discrimination": null,
  "usage_note": "Shake hands, hold hands, and clap your hands take the plural even when each person uses only one hand. You shake hands with someone or shake someone's hand. On the right hand and on the left hand, meaning on that side, are old-fashioned; today people say on the right or on the right-hand side.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "They shook hand and sat down.",
      "correct": "They shook hands and sat down.",
      "note": "The fixed phrase is always plural: shake hands, hold hands."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I made it by my hands.",
      "correct": "I made it by hand.",
      "note": "By hand has no possessive and no plural; the alternative is with my own hands."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "Can you give me hand?",
      "correct": "Can you give me a hand?",
      "note": "A hand meaning help is countable and needs a."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [
    {
      "slug": "finger-n",
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "slug": "wrist-n",
      "note": null
    }
  ],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "In many languages one word covers both the hand and the arm, or the border between them is drawn differently; English hand ends at the wrist. The help, control, clock, worker, and card senses are separate words almost everywhere, so each needs its own translation.",
    "grammar": "English uses a possessive, not the, with body parts: wash your hands, hold my hand. Several fixed uses are plural even for one hand each: shake hands, hold hands, clap your hands. The help sense is countable and singular: a hand, never hands.",
    "culture": "In most English-speaking countries adults shake hands when they are introduced and often when they part; a firm, short handshake is expected. Raising a hand is the normal way to ask to speak in a class or a meeting.",
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The vowel is the short a of cat. The plural hands ends in the cluster ndz, which learners often reduce to ns or nz; keep the d."
  },
